The order comes from `.sorted().reversed()`, i.e. lexicographic ascending then reversed, and the very comment at lines 344-347 states the premise that makes lexicographic order wrong: "Both levels are zero-padded, so directory names sort by time; the comparison is still done on parsed integers rather than on the strings, because \"9\" and \"09\" are both real names for September in the wild and only the numbers compare correctly." With a 1-digit name the reversed string order is ["9","12","11","10"] (String sort puts "10" < "11" < "12" < "9"), so September is visited first and `break`s the whole month loop for that year. `Int(month)` only converts; it never re-sorts.

